description:
A Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission (FWC) - Florida Boater Improvement Program (FBIP) grant application request for the proposed Harbour Pointe Regional Boat Ramp was approved by the BOCC on February 18, 2025. The application requested $250,000.00 from FWC-FBIP to offset the cost of the project’s design and permitting effort. The application was submitted on May 22, 2025, and on July 2, 2025, Port, Inlet & Beaches staff were notified that the grant application had been successfully ranked, and an award was forthcoming.
FWC - FBIP Grant #25050 was ultimately proffered to county staff for the design and permitting phase of the Harbour Pointe Regional Boat Ramp Project. The reimbursement grant provides $250,000.00 towards the proposed effort, which requires a local match of $707,774.28 to cover eligible project elements negotiated with FWC staff; match requirements which have been previously identified and secured for the ongoing project.
The proposed grant requires that the boat ramp plans include:
• Four (4) concrete boat ramp lanes;
• Five (5) floating docks with entry gangways;
• Approximately 420 feet of day-use mooring slips and staging dock adjacent to the ramp;
• Adequate storm water detention and treatment system to service the facility;
• A permanent restroom facility;
• Paved trailer and standard parking spaces; and
• An upland launch staging lane capable of accommodating multiple users simultaneously.
Section 3 of the agreement also allows retroactive reimbursement for efforts started within 60 days of FWC's execution of this agreement; the intent being, to cover design and permitting efforts that began on August 1, 2025.

In 2021, the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) allocated $5 billion in HOME-American Rescue Plan (HOME-ARP) funds to participating jurisdictions nationwide through the American Rescue Plan Act. The primary objective of HOME-ARP is to assist individuals and families experiencing or at risk of homelessness by funding the development of affordable rental housing, tenant-based rental assistance (TBRA), supportive services, and non-congregate shelter (NCS) projects.
As the lead entity for the St. Lucie County HOME Consortium, St. Lucie County was awarded $3,485,638 in HOME-ARP funds. HOME-ARP funds must primarily benefit individuals and families from the following HUD-defined Qualifying Populations:
Individuals or families experiencing homelessness;
Those at risk of homelessness;
Individuals or families fleeing or attempting to flee domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault, stalking, or human trafficking;
Other families requiring services or housing assistance to prevent homelessness;
Other populations at greatest risk of housing instability.
To determine local priorities, the County conducted a public consultation process as required by HUD, engaging the Continuum of Care (CoC), domestic violence and homeless service providers, veterans’ groups, public housing agencies, and fair housing and disability rights advocates. This was followed by a public hearing, a community needs assessment, and a gaps analysis, which identified the development of a non-congregate shelter as a top funding priority in St. Lucie County’s adopted HOME-ARP Allocation Plan.
To implement this priority, the County issued Request for Proposals (RFP No. 24-086) on July 28, 2024, seeking qualified nonprofit partners for the development and operation of a non-congregate shelter. The solicitation was distributed via DemandStar to 290 vendors, with 25 downloads. One proposal was received, from GraceWay Village, Inc., by the submission deadline of October 10, 2024.
GraceWay Village proposes the construction of the Family Restorative Shelter, a 10,150-square-foot facility to be built at 1780 Hartman Road in Fort Pierce. The shelter will include:10 private family units, each with a private bathroom;
Classrooms, communal areas, laundry, and administrative offices;
On-site case management and 24/7 staffing;
Transitional stays (up to 18 months) for families with minor children.
Key strengths of the proposal include:Full site control and debt-free land ownership;
Completed schematic design and project readiness;
$1,456,010 in committed matching funds (approx. 45.6% of project cost);
Partnerships with Treasure Coast Homeless Services Council, New Horizons, and United Against Poverty;
A trauma-informed, family-centered operating model with savings incentives and community sponsorships.
The project is consistent with HUD guidance and 24 CFR §92.206, which outlines eligible uses of HOME-ARP for acquisition, demolition, hard and soft development costs, and replacement reserves (but not operations or conversion to permanent housing). The facility will be subject to a 15-year restricted use period and must remain compliant with HUD habitability standards throughout.
GraceWay Village’s proposal was the sole response but met all eligibility and quality criteria. Based on the completeness, leverage, and alignment with County priorities, staff recommends awarding RFP No. 24-086 to GraceWay Village, Inc. in an amount not to exceed $2,962,792.30.
Prior to the award of this bid, an environmental assessment (EA) was required to be completed in accordance with the HUD 24 CFR Part 58. HOME-ARP funds must be fully expended by September 30, 2030.

