description:
In 2021, the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) allocated $5 billion in HOME-American Rescue Plan (HOME-ARP) funds to participating jurisdictions under the American Rescue Plan Act. The primary purpose of HOME-ARP is to address the needs of individuals and families experiencing homelessness or housing instability by funding the development of affordable housing, non-congregate shelter, rental assistance, and supportive services.
As the lead entity for the St. Lucie County HOME Consortium, St. Lucie County was originally awarded $3,480,403 in HOME-ARP funds. In May 2025, HUD notified the County of an administrative error identified during a national quality control review, which had resulted in the County being underfunded by $5,235. HUD has since issued a revised grant agreement reflecting a corrected total allocation of $3,485,638.
This amendment requires formal acceptance by the Board and execution of the amended grant agreement. Additionally, the County will be required to update its approved HOME-ARP Allocation Plan to reflect the increased funding, in accordance with HUD regulations.
HOME-ARP funds may be used for the following eligible activities:Development and preservation of affordable rental housing (homeownership activities are not eligible);
Tenant-Based Rental Assistance (TBRA);
Supportive services, including homeless prevention and housing counseling;
Purchase and development of non-congregate shelter (NCS) units (operational expenses are not eligible).
HOME-ARP funds must primarily benefit individuals and families who fall into one or more HUD-defined qualifying populations:Individuals or families experiencing homelessness;
Those at risk of homelessness;
Persons fleeing or attempting to flee domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault, stalking, or human trafficking;
Other families requiring services or housing assistance to prevent homelessness;
Other populations at greatest risk of housing instability.
As required by HUD, the County consulted with the local Continuum of Care (CoC), homeless and domestic violence service providers, veterans’ organizations, public housing agencies, and fair housing and disability rights advocates in the development of its allocation plan. The County also conducted a public hearing and completed a needs assessment and gaps analysis to evaluate the size and unmet needs of the qualifying populations.
As a result of these findings, the County’s approved HOME-ARP Allocation Plan identifies the creation of a non-congregate shelter as a top funding priority. To implement this activity, the County issued a Request for Proposals (RFP No. 24-086) in July 2024 to solicit proposals from eligible nonprofit entities for the construction and operation of a non-congregate shelter facility for families. Staff is currently completing the required HUD Environmental Assessment for the selected site. The assessment is expected to be finalized within the next 60 days. Upon its completion, staff will return to the Board with a recommendation to enter into negotiations with GraceWay Village for the development of the shelter.
Funding must be fully expended by ​September 30, 2030.

In 2003, the Board approved Resolution 03-108 and State Highway Lighting, Maintenance, and Compensation Agreement C03-04-311 with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT) to receive reimbursement for maintenance and operation of lighting on the State Highway System. The Florida Department of Transportation is updating these agreements to promote uniformity in language and compensation terms across the state.
In 2021, the Board adopted Resolution 21-366 approving the updated agreement. However, the agreement was halted by FDOT, and never went into effect. Notable additions in the 2021 agreement included:An annual compensation rate escalation of 3%;
Modifications of the number of lights maintained in "Exhibit A" must be in writing and signed by both parties;
The addition of an agreement term of seven (7) years, and terms for cancellation of the agreement;
Invoicing to FDOT must occur between May 1 and June 15 each year;
Greater emphasis on electronic recordkeeping;
E-Verify system for employment;
Public records transfer obligations upon completion;
Separate provision for Task Work Order issuance;
Clearer definitions and language modernization; and
Certification that the agreement text is unaltered.
FDOT is requesting the County execute the new agreement again with an updated resolution. The only changes since 2021 include the addition of the FEID Number to page 1, and updating the total compensation amount in Section 2 and compensation rates in Exhibit A to reflect the current rates. In accordance with the terms of the new agreement, FDOT shall compensate St. Lucie County a sum of $249,053.44 for operation and maintenance of 716 street lights during fiscal year 2026. The per light, unit rate of $347.84 shall increase by 3% each fiscal year.
